<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melqart</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melqart was the chief god of the Phoenician city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, whose name means 'king of the city'. Worshipped as protector of the city and its colonies, he had an annual festival of awakening, and his cult spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and Gades in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, and coins of Kition and other cities show the god as Heracles-Melqart.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melqart.jpg" alt="Melqart" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Testa_di_herakles-melqart,_500-480_ac_ca,_da_cipro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
